<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  14.2 Limits and Continuity in Higher Dimensions
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-limits-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Limits and Continuity in Higher Dimensions</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Explain what it means for
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> to exist, and why the value must be
the same along every path of approach.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate limits of functions of two variables using direct substitution,
algebraic simplification, and the limit laws.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Show that a limit does not exist by finding two paths along which the
function approaches different values.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Decide whether a function of two variables is continuous at a point, and
identify the points at which a piecewise-defined function fails to be
continuous.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
For <m>y = f(x)</m>, we could ask what value the function approaches when
<m>x</m> approaches <m>a \in \R</m>, and there were two possible
directions (from the left and from the right) to approach <m>x = a</m>,
which we denoted by <m>\lim_{x\to a^-} f(x)</m> and
<m>\lim_{x\to a^+} f(x)</m>. If
<m>\lim_{x\to a^-} f(x) = \lim_{x\to a^+} f(x) = L \in \R</m>, then
<m>\lim_{x\to a} f(x) = L</m>.
</p>
<p>
In two variables the situation is richer, because there are infinitely
many directions, and indeed infinitely many curved paths, along which a
point can approach <m>(a,b)</m>. This section makes that precise and
develops the tools for computing such limits, and for showing that a
limit fails to exist.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lim-two-variable">
<title>Limits of Two-Variable Functions</title>

<p>
Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> that we studied in the previous
section. Below are some key points regarding the limit of such a
function.
</p>

<p>
<alert>I.</alert> There are infinitely many ways of approaching a point
<m>(a,b)</m>. A few such paths are drawn in green in
<xref ref="fig-lim-paths"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-paths">
<caption>
A few of the infinitely many paths along which a point can approach
<m>(a,b)</m>. The paths may be straight lines or curves, and they may
arrive from any direction.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-paths">
<diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="12">
<definition>p1(t) = (1.55 + 0.95*sin(2.6*t), 2.55 - 1.55*t)</definition>
<definition>p2(t) = (1.55 - 1.7*t + 0.35*t^2, 0.35 + 0.65*t)</definition>
<definition>p3(t) = (1.55 + 1.85*t, 1.0 + 0.72*t^2)</definition>
<definition>p4(t) = (1.55 + 1.9*t - 0.55*t^2, 1.0 - 0.95*t^2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.55,-0.5,4.2,3.2)">
<axes at="lp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c1" function="p1" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c2" function="p2" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c3" function="p3" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c4" function="p4" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<vector at="lp-a1" v="(-0.16,-0.30)" tail="(1.71,1.30)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="lp-a2" v="(0.30,-0.12)" tail="(1.25,1.12)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<line at="lp-hx" endpoints="((0,1.0),(1.55,1.0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="lp-hy" endpoints="((1.55,0),(1.55,1.0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="lp-P" p="(1.55,1.0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="lp-P-lab" anchor="(1.62,1.03)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>(a,b)</m>
</label>
<label at="lp-a" anchor="(1.55,-0.06)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
<m>a</m>
</label>
<label at="lp-b" anchor="(-0.06,1.0)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>b</m>
</label>
<label at="lp-x" anchor="(4.0,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="lp-y" anchor="(-0.04,3.05)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate plane with the point a comma b marked in orange, located by dashed lines dropped to the two axes. Four green curves of different shapes come in towards that point from different directions, two of them carrying arrowheads, showing that a point may approach a comma b along infinitely many different paths.">
  <annotation ref="lp-P" text="The point a comma b that is being approached."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c1" text="One path approaching the point from above, curving as it comes in."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c2" text="A second path approaching from the upper left."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c3" text="A third path approaching from the right along a curve."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c4" text="A fourth path approaching from below. For the limit to exist, the function must approach the same value along every one of these paths."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>II.</alert> The limit
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> exists if, regardless of the path
taken to approach the point <m>(a,b)</m>, the value of the function
<m>f(x,y)</m> approaches the same value.
</p>

<p>
<alert>III.</alert> To show that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist, it suffices to show
that the value of the function approaches two different values for two
different paths taken to move towards <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<alert>IV.</alert> Some useful properties of limits are stated in
<xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-lim-properties">
<title>Properties of Limits</title>
<statement>
<p>
The following rules hold if <m>L</m>, <m>M</m>, and <m>k</m> are real
numbers and
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y) = L
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} g(x,y) = M.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>1. Sum Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)+g(x,y)\bigr)
= L + M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>2. Difference Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)-g(x,y)\bigr)
= L - M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>3. Product Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)\cdot g(x,y)\bigr)
= L \cdot M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>4. Constant Multiple Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} k f(x,y) = kL</m>
for any number <m>k</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>5. Quotient Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \frac{f(x,y)}{g(x,y)}
= \frac{L}{M}</m>, provided <m>M \neq 0</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>6. Power Rule:</alert> If <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> are integers with
no common factors and <m>s \neq 0</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)\bigr)^{r/s}
= L^{r/s}</m>, provided <m>L^{r/s}</m> is a real number. (If <m>s</m> is
even, we assume that <m>L \gt 0</m>.)
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-lim-substitution">
<title>Evaluating a Limit by Direct Substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} + \cos(xy).
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Note that we can use the direct substitution property to write
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} = \frac{2}{2\pi} = \frac{1}{\pi}
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \cos(xy) = \cos(4\pi) = 1.
</md>
Then we use the sum rule to write
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} + \cos(xy) = \frac{1}{\pi} + 1.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-lim-dne">
<title>A Limit That Does Not Exist</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. Does
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> exist?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by approaching the point <m>(0,0)</m> along the line
<m>y = 0</m>. Note that along the line <m>y = 0</m>, we have
<md>
f(x,0) = \frac{0}{x^2+0^2} = 0,
</md>
and hence
<md>
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = 0.
</md>
Similarly,
<md>
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } x = 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Let us next see what happens when approaching the point <m>(0,0)</m>
along the line <m>y = x</m>. We have
<md>
f(x,x) = \frac{x^2}{x^2+x^2} = \frac{1}{2},
\qquad \text{(note that } x \neq 0 \text{)}
</md>
and hence
<md>
f(x,y) \to \frac{1}{2} \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = x.
</md>
</p>
<p>
As was shown above and is depicted in
<xref ref="fig-lim-three-paths"/>, when approaching along different
paths the function's value approaches different values: zero along
<m>y = 0</m> and <m>x = 0</m>, and <m>\frac12</m> along <m>y = x</m>.
Therefore we can conclude that the limit does not exist.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-three-paths">
<caption>
Three paths of approach to the origin for
<m>f(x,y) = \frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. The function is constantly <m>0</m>
along the two axes and constantly <m>\frac12</m> along the line
<m>y=x</m>, so no single value is approached and the limit does not
exist.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-three-paths">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="12">
<definition>diag(t) = (t, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.4,-2.2,2.6,2.4)">
<axes at="tp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="tp-diag" function="diag" domain="(-1.95,1.95)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="tp-xaxis" endpoints="((-2.1,0),(2.1,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="tp-yaxis" endpoints="((0,-1.9),(0,1.9))" stroke="#D55E00"
      thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="tp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="tp-diag-lab" anchor="(1.72,1.86)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=x</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-diag" anchor="(1.05,0.62)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-x" anchor="(1.85,-0.12)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-y" anchor="(-0.14,1.62)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-x" anchor="(2.42,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="tp-y" anchor="(-0.04,2.25)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The x and y axes and the diagonal line y equals x, all drawn in orange and all passing through the origin. The two axes are labelled f equals zero and the diagonal is labelled f equals one half, showing that the function takes different constant values along different lines through the origin.">
  <annotation ref="tp-xaxis" text="The path along the x axis, where y equals zero. Along it the function is constantly zero."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-yaxis" text="The path along the y axis, where x equals zero. Along it the function is also constantly zero."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-diag" text="The path along the line y equals x. Along it the function is constantly one half, a different value, so the limit at the origin does not exist."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-O" text="The origin, the point being approached."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-lim-simplify">
<title>Evaluating a Limit by Simplifying</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\frac{x^2}{2} - xy + \frac{y^2}{2}}{x^2 - y^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by simplifying the fraction. The numerator is
<m>\frac12\left(x^2 - 2xy + y^2\right) = \frac12 (x-y)^2</m> and the
denominator factors as <m>(x-y)(x+y)</m>, so
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\frac{x^2}{2} - xy + \frac{y^2}{2}}{x^2 - y^2}
= \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{1}{2}\,\frac{(x-y)^2}{(x-y)(x+y)}
= \frac{1}{2} \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x-y}{x+y} = 0.
</md>
The cancellation is legitimate because <m>x \neq y</m> along the
approach, and the final limit is evaluated by direct substitution, giving
<m>\frac{1-1}{1+1} = 0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lim-continuity">
<title>Continuity</title>

<definition xml:id="def-continuity-two-variables">
<title>Continuity at a Point and on the Domain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>f(x,y)</m> is <term>continuous at the point</term>
<m>(x_0,y_0)</m> if
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>f</m> is defined at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y)</m> exists,
</p>
<p>
<alert>3.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
<p>
A function is <term>continuous</term> if it is continuous at every point
of its domain.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-cont-2xy">
<title>Continuity Everywhere Except at the Origin</title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2xy}{x^2+y^2} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is continuous everywhere except at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The function is continuous at any point <m>(x,y) \neq (0,0)</m>. This is
because, at such points, it is a rational function whose denominator is
non-zero, and therefore, using the quotient rule of
<xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>, the limit of <m>f(x,y)</m> is equal to
the value of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
<p>
To show that <m>f(x,y)</m> is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>, we
will show that its limit does not exist at this point. Let us first
consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along <m>y = 0</m>. Since
along such a line we have <m>f(x,0) = \frac{0}{x^2+0^2} = 0</m>, we can
say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-2xy-path1" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = 0.
</md>
Next we consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along
<m>y = x</m>. Since along such a line we have
<m>f(x,x) = \frac{2x^2}{x^2+x^2} = 1</m>, we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-2xy-path2" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 1 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = x.
</md>
From <xref ref="eq-cont-2xy-path1"/> and
<xref ref="eq-cont-2xy-path2"/>, we conclude that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist, and hence the function
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cont-xy2">
<title>A Discontinuity Detected by a Curved Path</title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{xy^2}{x^2+y^4} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
To show that <m>f(x,y)</m> is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>, we
will show that its limit does not exist at this point.
</p>
<p>
Let us first consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along
<m>y = mx</m>, where <m>m \in \R</m>. Since along such a line we have
<m>f(x,mx) = \dfrac{m^2x^3}{x^2+m^4x^4}</m>, which simplifies to
<m>\dfrac{m^2 x}{1 + m^4 x^2}</m>, we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-xy2-path1" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = mx.
</md>
Notice that this holds for <em>every</em> slope <m>m</m>: approaching
along any straight line gives the limit <m>0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Next, we consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along the curve
<m>x = y^2</m>, which gives
<m>f(y^2,y) = \dfrac{y^4}{y^4+y^4} = \dfrac12</m>, and we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-xy2-path2" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to \frac{1}{2} \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the curve } x = y^2.
</md>
From <xref ref="eq-cont-xy2-path1"/> and
<xref ref="eq-cont-xy2-path2"/>, we conclude that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist and hence the function
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
<p>
This example is worth pausing over: checking every straight line through
the origin is <em>not</em> enough to establish that a limit exists.
<xref ref="fig-lim-parabola-path"/> shows the family of straight paths
together with the parabola that detects the discontinuity.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-parabola-path">
<caption>
Every straight line through the origin gives the limit <m>0</m>, but
along the parabola <m>x = y^2</m> the function is constantly
<m>\frac12</m>. A limit must agree along <em>all</em> paths, not merely
all straight ones.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-parabola">
<diagram dimensions="(380,320)" margins="12">
<definition>par(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.85,2.6,1.85)">
<axes at="pp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<line at="pp-l1" endpoints="((-1.25,-0.42),(1.25,0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l2" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.0),(1.25,1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l3" endpoints="((-1.25,0.42),(1.25,-0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l4" endpoints="((-1.25,1.0),(1.25,-1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l5" endpoints="((-1.25,1.6),(1.25,-1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l6" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.6),(1.25,1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="pp-par" function="par" domain="(-1.35,1.35)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="pp-par-lab" anchor="(1.9,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>x=y^2:\; f=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-lines-lab" anchor="(-1.35,-1.55)" alignment="sw" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>y=mx:\; f\to 0</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-x" anchor="(2.45,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pp-y" anchor="(-0.04,1.72)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate plane with six grey straight lines of different slopes all passing through the origin, and one thick orange parabola opening to the right, also passing through the origin. The grey lines are labelled f tends to zero and the parabola is labelled f equals one half.">
  <annotation ref="pp-l2" text="One of the straight paths y equals m x through the origin. Along every such line the function tends to zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-par" text="The parabola x equals y squared. Along this curved path the function is constantly one half, which is why the limit fails to exist even though every straight line gives zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-O" text="The origin, where the function is defined to be zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>Alternative method.</alert> Alternatively, we can show that
<m>f(x,y) \to \frac12</m> as <m>(x,y) \to (0,0)</m> along the curve
<m>x = y^2</m>, and since <m>f(0,0) = 0 \neq \frac12</m>, we can conclude
that the function is not continuous at the origin by condition
<alert>3</alert> of <xref ref="def-continuity-two-variables"/>: even if
the limit existed, it would have to equal <m>f(0,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
